<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet component = "cur" xml:id = "wksht-quad-methods" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Comparing Methods for Solving Quadratic Equations</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li><title>Dates</title>
                    <p>Assigned Date: October 16, 2026</p>
                    <p>Due Date: October 21, 2026</p>
                </li>
                <li><title>Objective</title>
                    <p>
                        Being able to choose an efficient method to solve a quadratic equation is a skill that will be of great benefit as you progress into higher mathematics. This worksheet asks you to compare methods and explore the differences and benefits of each. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li> <title>Criteria for Success</title>
                    <p>
                        Your assignment is due to your instructor by the date listed above. Your instructor will be assessing completeness and correctness for each question.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </introduction>

<page>

<paragraphs>
    <title>Problems</title>

<exercise workspace="0.2in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>x^2 + 7x = 18</m>, explaining your steps.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using the principle of zero products.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using completing the square.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which way was easier? Why?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

<exercise workspace="0.2in">
    <introduction>
        <p>
            Solve the equation <m>7x^2 = 63</m>, explaining your steps.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using the principle of square roots.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Using the quadratic formula.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Which way was easier? Why?
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</paragraphs>
</page>

<page>
<exercise>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            List the four methods that we have used for solving quadratic equations. Then, for each of the four methods, determine which of the statements below apply to it. A given statement below may apply to more than one method. For each of the statements, provide a brief explanation of why it applies. For example: The statement <q>Best for solving quadratics with one term</q> would be listed under <q>Principle of Square Roots</q> and a reason would be: <q>Any quadratic with only one term would look like <m>ax^2 = 0</m>, dividing by a and taking the square root of both sides to get x, would be fast and easy.</q>
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Best for solving quadratics with <m>b=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Best for solving quadratics with <m>c=0</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Always applies.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Best for solving quadratics with large coefficients.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Won't apply if the solutions are complex.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Equation must be factorable.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The method I would choose if I were stuck.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
    <task>
        <statement>
            <p>
                My favorite method.
            </p>
        </statement>
    </task>
</exercise>

</page>


</worksheet>